.TH HALT 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
halt \- stop the computer, turning it off
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B halt
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B halt
flushes its output and asks the kernel to turn the system off, as
.BR poweroff (1)
does.
Every process ends and its open files are closed, so what was written
reaches the disks; then the machine turns off.
Arguments are ignored.
.PP
To start again, use the power button, or
.BR reboot (1)
instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
On success
.B halt
does not return.
If the kernel refuses, it prints the reason on standard error and
exits with 1.
.SH NOTES
Linux's
.B halt
stops the CPUs and leaves the power on. This computer's board has no such
state: a CPU stops with its power on only after a fault, and a fault turns
the machine off (its power light blinking amber; why is in
.I /sys/firmware/last_shutdown
after the next boot). So
.B halt
powers off, as
.B halt \-p
does.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR poweroff (1),
.BR reboot (1),
.BR kill (1)
